cause you're not an  elephant in that one yeah I don't  understand what that one matically at  all that what I think was probably my  least favorite of them just because it  was essentially a 3d art tool and  maneuvering the trunk of the elephant  around and you're using that just kind  of like draw objects in a 3d space and  there were a lot of tools you could  change colors you could add effects to  it and stuff you could rotate the object  in 3d but I feel like if you're not very  artistically inclined like I am there's  no real incentive to play that one sure  and that was kind of like the the first  minigame you can do with that then there  was sort of those like rube  goldberg style puzzle machines that you  can also play with that toy con each toy  con has like a secondary minigame we  didn't really get to try those though  yeah only tried one of each from what  they it's just though it seems like the  secondary minigames are where you're  going to have more fun like the first  one seems to introduce you to the  mechanics of how this toy con works the  second one really explores like having  more fun with it for lack of a better  term the Rube Goldberg style puzzles  were much more complex and you could  actually build your own and that's way  more in-depth than just you know a  drawing kit yeah the next one we played  probably had the least amount of depth I  would say which was the Frog the least  amount depth as far as gameplay but it  does have a really cool kind of extra 40  experience yeah I think like sensory  wise that one was probably the most  memorable of the bunch because that one  was controlled with the what a toy con  they called the wind pedal it was a  pedal that you'd lay down on the floor  and when you'd press it with your foot  it would just kind of like cause this  fan to flap up and blow air into your  face so you're pressing down the wind  pedal to make this frog in the game leap  upward and as you do that you'd feel the  wind in your face so it was very trippy  to feel the wind as you're seeing the  Frog jump the final one we played of the  toy cons was the camera yes that one was  probably my favorite  I'd have to say your object just to look  around and snap pictures of various  animals you you literally twist like  what would be a focus ring of a real  camera you snap the shoulder button of  the toy con that's inserted on the side  to take your picture and that felt  really cool that one was like the only  minigame that we got to play that  actually tried to approach a more  realistic visual style yeah so it was  very neat to look around the water and  see all the different animals swimming  around and in general it all just kind  of reminded me of Pokemon snap made me  want that even more this is literally  the perfect device for playing a new  Pokemon staff game it is perfectly  suited for it please Nintendo make that  happen  it would be a shame if that doesn't  happen now after we were done with this  toy cons we got to play in what they  call the VR Plaza we  is paired with the LeBeau garage or the  toy car garage rather but it seems to be  much more in-depth than the initial  garage you can literally make VR games  of your own you're not just programming  the joy con and toy con functions you're  actually programming these stages and  what the characters are doing on the  screen there are 64 minigames included  in the plaza made by the lab o  developers any one of which you can  literally just open up and then under  the hood see the logic they programmed  and make your own version of that game  or tweak what they made or make  something completely original we didn't  actually get to tinker around with the  underlying mechanics behind the games  but we got to look at a few of them and  it seems very like surprisingly flexible  to me there's a lot of different kinds  of games you could make we got to try  out this one little platformer we played  like a Smash Brothers ripoff yeah very a  